  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper aims to develop and evaluate a straightforward, automatable, high-yield and high-molar-activity method for PET tracer development by directly radiofluorinating α-bromo-α-fluoroalkane precursors with no-carrier-added [18F]fluoride to synthesize 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethylalkanes.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a PET-relevant fluorinated motif is valuable but existing access is limited, and a plausible [18F]fluoride substitution strategy is identified, the paper-level aim is to develop and evaluate a practical radiosynthetic method for that motif."];
  "Node_Case_1" [label="(16,0,0) Currently the highly polarized carbon-hydrogen bond in the difluoromethyl group endows it with the capability to act as a hydrogen bond donor."];
  "Node_Concl_1" [label="(15,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the difluoromethyl group acts as a hydrogen bond donor and stable bioisostere, therefore it has the potential to confer distinct advantages in biomolecule designs."];
  "Node_Concl_2" [label="(23,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given the difluoromethyl group confers distinct advantages, therefore the demand for novel methods to access the 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethyl moiety continues to grow."];
  "Node_Concl_3" [label="(38,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given the growing demand and limitations of existing methods, therefore there is a clear need for simple and efficient broad-scope methodologies for labeling α,α-difluoromethylalkanes with fluorine-18 at high molar activity."];
  "Node_Concl_4" [label="(39,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given the need for high molar activity 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethylalkanes, therefore we envisioned that [18F]fluoride treatment of an α-fluoroalkane possessing a leaving group on the α-carbon might provide the desired product."];
  "Node_Knowledge_2" [label="(0,0,0) If a chemical group confers distinct advantages, there is growing demand to label it with Fluorine-18 for PET imaging."];
  "Node_Knowledge_3" [label="(32,0,0) If demand continues to grow but existing methods provide mediocre molar activities, then few effective methods exist for the radiosynthesis of an 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethylalkane."];
  "Node_Knowledge_4" [label="(0,0,0) If there is a clear need for high molar activity 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethylalkanes, treating an α-fluoroalkane possessing a leaving group on the α-carbon with [18F]fluoride might provide the desired product."];
  "Node_Knowledge_5" [label="(0,0,0) If an envisioned treatment is successfully implemented, it results in an effective method for direct radiofluorination."];
  "Node_Rule_1" [label="(17,0,0) If difluoromethyl groups can serve as metabolically stable bioisosteres, then they can be used to improve pharmacokinetic properties."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) The envisioned treatment of an α-fluoroalkane bearing a leaving group on the α-carbon with [18F]fluoride motivates developing and evaluating a direct radiofluorination approach to access 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethylalkanes."];
